<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catalan Courts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1283">1283</a>–<a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Corts Catalanes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catalan Courts were the parliament of the Principality of Catalonia, made up of three estates: clergy, nobility and royal towns. Formalised under Peter III, who in 1283 promised to summon them yearly, they passed laws together with the king and voted taxes, until they were abolished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a> of 1716.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catalan-courts.jpg" alt="Catalan Courts" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cort_general_del_Principat_de_Catalunya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Courts grew out of the comital court of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, of about 1000, modelled on the Frankish curia regis, and out of the assemblies of the Peace and Truce, which met from 1021 to end wars and feudal violence. Townspeople first took part in such an assembly in 1192. The Royal Court of 1214, called at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> by the papal legate Cardinal Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> after King Peter fell at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a>, received the oath of the six-year-old James I, and the Court of 1218 is counted as the first General Court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Each estate formed an arm. The ecclesiastical arm, under the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, abbots and cathedral chapters; the military arm, under the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardona">Cardona</a>, the titled nobles, knights and lords with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>; and the royal arm, under the Chief Councilor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, the síndics of the towns under royal jurisdiction. Barcelona sent five, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> three and most towns one, each bound by an imperative mandate from its municipality. Laws proposed by the king were called Constitucions, those of the estates Capítols de Cort. Majorca sent its representatives to these Courts, which often met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1289 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a> the Courts appointed a standing delegation to collect the tax they granted the king, and in 1358-1359, under King Peter IV, they named twelve deputies with powers over taxation. This body, the Deputation of the General or Generalitat, was headed by Berenguer de Cruïlles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>. The Courts of 1480, the first of Ferdinand II, approved the Constitution of the Observance, which bound the king to respect the constitutions and let the Generalitat revoke unconstitutional orders.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> the Courts were summoned less and less. The Courts of 1626, at which Philip IV sought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-arms">Union of Arms</a> of his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Gaspar de Guzmán, Count-Duke of Olivares</a>, were never concluded, and the tensions led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reapers-war">Reapers' War</a> of 1640-1652. The last Court, under the Habsburg claimant Charles III in 1705-1706, extended individual rights such as the secrecy of correspondence. Between 1214 and 1705 the Courts were convened 104 times, and the Parliament of Catalonia, founded in 1932, is considered their successor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “General Court of Catalonia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Catalan Corts</b>, the parliament of clergy, nobility and royal towns, took definite form under Peter III, who in 1283 promised to summon them every year; legislation required the consent of king and Corts together, an early form of contractual government that Catalans called pactism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
